task: split-url-named
agent: fable51-core
started: 2026-09-12
branch: feature/split-url-named
worktree: ../okay-wt-spliturl
spec: BACKLOG `named-pairs-rest`, which this lane also CORRECTS

why: operator, 2026-09-12 — "так что ничего не нужно делать?". The
  question sent me back to check my own record, and it was wrong.
  `named-pairs-rest` says of the five remaining same-typed pairs:
  "All are private or module-local, so the blast radius is small".
  Four are. `Site.splitUrl` is PUBLIC:

    def splitUrl(url: String): (String, String)   // (path, query)

  A user of okay-script calls it and destructures positionally, and a
  swap gives them the query string as the path with nothing to catch
  it. That is the same shape as the two security pairs this repository
  named this morning, and it was dismissed on a claim I had not
  checked.

what:
  1. `def splitUrl(url: String): (path: String, query: String)`.
  2. its four call sites, all in Site.scala, move off position: three
     `val (path, query) = splitUrl(r.url)` and `pathOf`, which reads
     `._1` — a named tuple has no `_1`, so `pathOf` becomes
     `splitUrl(url).path`, which is what it meant all along.
  3. CORRECT the BACKLOG entry: four private, one public and now done,
     with the sentence that was wrong marked rather than quietly
     edited — the claim it made is the reason it was not taken.

predict: `._1` is the one thing that will not compile, which is fine
  because it is also the line that most wanted a name. If anything
  outside this repository called `splitUrl` positionally it still
  compiles, since a positional destructure of a named tuple binds by
  position — so this is not a breaking change for a caller, only a
  better one for a reader.
